architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0007"
  title: Numeric error codes for parse errors, mirrored per language
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-07-12"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: [[[
    SyonError (Rust) and syon.Error (Go) carried only a Forbidden/Syntax
    kind plus a human-readable message. There was no stable, programmatic
    way to ask "is this specifically a duplicate-key error?" without
    string-matching the message, which is fragile across wording changes
    and impossible to keep in sync across three independently-implemented
    parsers (see docs/decisions/0004-independent-go-implementation.syon).

    A three-digit numeric code was requested, banded by block: 1-99 for
    general/pre-block errors, 101-199 for Block 1 (records), 201-299 for
    Block 2, 301-399 for Block 3, with similar problems in different
    blocks sharing the same low two digits (e.g. 112, 212, 312 all mean
    "anchor used where forbidden," in a record, a literal block, and a
    fence body respectively).

    Confirmed directly: this table reuses the phase1 tool's block
    numbering (Block 2 equals literal blocks, Block 3 equals document
    fences), the same numbering already adopted by
    docs/decisions/0006-phase1-block-numbering.syon and for the same
    reason -- so a phase1.report.syon and a parse error agree with each
    other, at the cost of disagreeing with spec/02-grammar.md's opposite
    numbering.

    Writing the Rust preflight scan test coverage for these codes
    surfaced a real, previously-undiscovered bug: an unterminated
    ` ``` ` document fence (opened but never closed) was silently
    accepted by the Rust parser, which absorbed everything after the
    open fence as its body with no error. Go already rejected this
    correctly. Confirmed directly to fix this now rather than only
    document it.
    ]]]
  decision: [[[
    Add a code: ErrorCode field to every parse error in all three
    implementations:

    - Rust: syon_parser::ErrorCode, a fieldless enum with explicit u16
      discriminants matching the table in spec/05-error-codes.md;
      SyonError::code() -> ErrorCode. SyonError's variants were changed
      from tuple structs carrying only a message to struct variants
      carrying { code, message }, since a single free-text message could
      no longer imply the code.
    - Go: syon.ErrorCode, an int-based named-constant type (syon.CodeAnchor,
      etc.) with the same numeric values; syon.Error gained a Code field.
    - Python: since PyO3 0.21 does not bridge a Rust enum into Python
      automatically, a hand-written mirror, syon.ErrorCode (a #[pyclass]
      fieldless enum), reusing the exact numeric discriminants and
      exposing Python-idiomatic UPPER_CASE member names via a
      #[pyo3(name = "...")] attribute per variant (PyO3 0.21 otherwise
      exposes the bare Rust identifier, e.g. Anchor, not ANCHOR). Parse
      errors are raised as syon.SyonError, a pyclass extending
      PyValueError so existing `except ValueError` callers keep working,
      carrying .code (a syon.ErrorCode) and .message (str), with __str__
      rendering `[SYON-{code}] {message}`.

    Fixed the unterminated-fence bug found while implementing this: the
    Rust preflight scan now tracks fence-open/fence-close state across
    lines the same way it already tracked in_literal for [[[ blocks, and
    raises UnterminatedFence if a fence is still open at end of input.
    ]]]
  consequences:
    positive:
      - Callers in all three languages can dispatch on a stable numeric code instead of matching message text, e.g. `if err.code() == ErrorCode::DuplicateKey`.
      - phase1.report.syon and a raised parse error now use the same block-numbering convention, rather than requiring a reader to hold two different numbering schemes in mind depending on which output they are looking at.
      - The unterminated-fence silent-acceptance bug is fixed with regression tests in both Rust and Go, closing a real gap where a truncated or malformed document could be silently misparsed as valid.
    negative:
      - Three more error-code tables (Rust enum, Go constants, Python pyclass) must now be kept in sync by hand alongside the three existing parser implementations -- another instance of the cross-implementation drift risk flagged by ADR 0004, concretely realized here as codes 311-318 (forbidden construct inside fence content) being reachable in Rust but not Go, since Rust's preflight scan does not exempt fence bodies from the same forbidden-construct scan applied to records, while Go treats fence content as opaque. This contradicts spec/03-semantics.md's claim that fence content is returned as a raw string and never parsed, and is left open as a tracked, documented gap rather than fixed here, per the same reasoning ADR 0006 gave for not fixing it: closing it needs fence content captured as raw text end to end (grammar, AST type, and Document.body's type), which is substantially larger than adding error codes.
      - SyonError's Rust variants changed shape (tuple to struct-with-fields), a breaking API change for any external caller pattern-matching on SyonError directly; no such caller exists in this repository today (ffi.rs only calls .to_string()).
      - Codes 211-218 (forbidden construct inside a literal block) and, in Go, 311-318 as well, are permanently reserved and unreachable given each implementation's current architecture -- they exist in the table for numbering symmetry with 111-118/311-318, not because they can ever be raised.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Follow spec/02-grammar.md's block numbering instead of phase1's
      reason: Rejected when asked directly -- error codes are meant to agree with phase1.report.syon's numbering, which was itself already confirmed intentional in ADR 0006, not with the grammar spec's opposite convention.
    -
      name: Auto-generate the Go and Python error-code tables from the Rust enum instead of hand-mirroring them
      reason: Would remove the sync-by-hand risk, but no code-generation tooling exists in this project yet (see ADR 0004's rejection of a similar idea for the Go parser itself); worth reconsidering if the tables drift in practice.
    -
      name: Leave the unterminated-fence bug undocumented for a later change
      reason: Rejected when asked directly -- fixing it now, alongside adding the FenceInfoStringMalformed/UnterminatedFence codes that made it easy to notice and test, was preferred over letting a known silent-acceptance bug persist.
